 there because while western pressure  sanctions from the us really is not  going to have much impact on the  general's decision-making  myanmar the generals included value  their membership in the association of  southeast asian nations they value that  membership more than they value their  membership in the united nations  so the generals will certainly listen to  what ajin has to say  but ajin has generally been a toothless  organization  now short term the best we can hope from  ajin is that they can get the generals  to commit  to no more deadly force on the  protesters if the generals commit to  that and actually stick with it  that would be a short-term  accomplishment but long-term for aging  to help come up with some sort of  workable solution that all sides can  agree on  is going to be a huge undertaking  because right now both sides are showing  little room for compromise on their  demands  it's clear dave um that protesting is  becoming more dangerous meantime on the  ground when we look at the situation  there  amid a crackdown that's been happening  in the past days  tell us a little bit more about how that  is impacting resistance efforts  yeah so you see some of the protesters  are out there now they've got some sort  of makeshift protection some of them are  wearing construction hard hats  goggles that you can buy in a hardware  store they've got sort of makeshift  shields but  you know against live rounds i'm not  sure how much protection that's really  going to  provide which is why a lot of the  demonstrators they've written on their  arms they've written their blood type  and the contact information for their  next of kin these demonstrators  they realize how dangerous this is not  only from the number of people who died  on sunday but also they know  the junta in past years where they've  shown no hesitation to gun down hundreds  of demonstrators at a time on the  streets  but some of these demonstrators have  said if necessary they are willing to  die for this cause  because they believe if they do not  stand up to this military government now  and fight back against this coup  they believe that any hope of having a  true democracy in this country will be  lost  for at least another generation that  sense that sense of resolve is really  incredible to witness journalist dave  grunibaum joining us  with the latest on the situation in  myanmar thank you
 

